// IRIW -- independent reads of independent writes, release-acquire throughout.
//
// Source: Arlt & Vafeiadis, "RGSep under Release/Acquire Consistency",
//         PACMPL 10 (OOPSLA2), Article 375, 2026. doi:10.1145/3839507, Section 1.
//
// The two readers may see the independent writes to x and y in opposite orders:
// RA is not multi-copy atomic. The paper uses it to motivate reasoning under
// weak memory; RGSep proves a trivial postcondition for it although it is not
// robust (Section 7).
//
// Reference verdicts:
//   the paper: RA allows a = c = 1 and b = d = 0.
//   herd7 7.58 on herd/IRIW.litmus, under the zoo's abstract-sc, abstract-tso,
//     ra, sra, wra and abstract-coherence cat models and herd's rc11.cat:
//     SC and TSO forbid; RA, SRA, WRA, Coherence and RC11 allow.
// The paper's registers are renamed a -> ra, b -> rb, c -> rc, d -> rd:
// MoRDor reads only names beginning with r as registers.
x := 0; y := 0;
{ x.store(1, rel) }
||| { ra := x.load(acq); rb := y.load(acq) }
||| { rc := y.load(acq); rd := x.load(acq) }
||| { y.store(1, rel) }
%%
allow (ra = 1 && rb = 0 && rc = 1 && rd = 0) [RA, SRA, WRA, Coherence, RC11]
forbid (ra = 1 && rb = 0 && rc = 1 && rd = 0) [SC, TSO]
